\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Topic shortcut and label equivalence.}
Three observations argue against a topic shortcut: unstable topic OOD (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207), scrambled labels within 3.9\,pp, and label-free MLM detection (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97).

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
ToxicChat (+18--42\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:human_data}) and cross-LLM experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) confirm transfer. Mixed-LLM 9-class maintains DD F1\,=\,0.818$\pm$0.096 (+53\,pp) while MLM collapses to vanilla level. A mixed-source training pilot preserves Qwen OOD detection with preliminary Llama transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}).

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
Classification supervision is associated with lower variance on synthetic OOD (4--20$\times$ observed range, 3 seeds); on ToxicChat this reverses (std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ 0.013). Frozen probing localizes MLM instability to Stage-2 (Appendix~\ref{app:frozen_verify}).

\paragraph{Encoder capacity and lexical analysis.}
Three encoders (125M--355M) differ in pretraining; same-architecture scaling is needed. TF-IDF collapses on ActorAttack (0.261 vs.\ 0.961 on DD).

\paragraph{Regularization mechanism.}
Frozen probing near-parity (AA gap 3.1\,pp), attack-dependent label sensitivity ($d \approx 0$ on DD/FITD, $d$\,=\,0.44 on ActorAttack at $n$\,=\,10), and mean pooling matching BiGRU ($|\Delta| < 1$\,pp) converge: classification DAPT provides robustness primarily through optimization regularization, with residual semantic contribution on structurally complex attacks, paralleling self-supervised results where training structure matters more than label content~\citep{chen2020simclr,grill2020byol}. Unlike random-label memorization~\citep{zhang2021understanding,maennel2020random}, random-label \emph{pretraining} here produces transferable representations, suggesting that classification structure regularizes the space. Three extensions emerge: MLM collapses under mixed-LLM training despite single-LLM effectiveness, frozen probing near-parity coexists with 8\,pp end-to-end gaps, and the hierarchy reverses on human data.

\paragraph{Two-axis generalization: attack-side vs.\ benign-side.}
OOD F1 figures throughout this paper reflect attack-side generalization under IID benign conditions: DAPT closes the attack-side gap (vanilla F1\,=\,0.28--0.31 $\rightarrow$ 0.97--1.0 on OOD families). The orthogonal challenge is benign-side distribution shift. On WildChat, 9-class FPR reaches 68.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:wildchat_fpr}); cross-LLM error decomposition confirms collapse is driven by false positives on unfamiliar benign text, not detection failure. A benign adaptation curve (Appendix~\ref{app:benign_mixed}) shows FPR drops from 68.1\% to 0\% with as few as 10 conversations (95\% CI upper 6.1\%, $n$\,=\,49) while all OOD F1 remains $\geq$\,0.953: the two axes are orthogonal and independently addressable.

\section{Conclusion}

Controlled ablation across thirteen encoder configurations decomposes multi-turn jailbreak generalization into two orthogonal axes. On the attack side, classification-based DAPT is the most robust adaptation for base-size encoders, providing robustness through optimization regularization rather than semantic learning; unsupervised pretraining collapses under mixed-LLM training. On the benign side, 10 target-domain benign conversations eliminate distributional false positives (WildChat FPR 68.1\%\,$\rightarrow$\,0\%) without detection degradation. Label sensitivity is attack-complexity-dependent (TOST-confirmed equivalence on DD/FITD at $n$\,=\,10; $d$\,=\,0.44 on ActorAttack); negative results on temporal order and label granularity further constrain which properties matter. Combined with 24.3$\times$ speedup over LLM-as-judge (Appendix~\ref{app:efficiency}), the two-axis decomposition yields a practical deployment recipe that requires no target-domain attack data.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All multi-turn findings derive from synthetic conversations generated by Qwen3-8B (350 training, 76--118 test per family); preliminary mixed-LLM training (Appendix~\ref{app:mixed_llm}) suggests this limitation is addressable. ToxicChat validates transfer to human-authored single-turn attacks (+18--42\,pp over vanilla), but no human-generated multi-turn jailbreak dataset exists. Whether the evidence hierarchy generalizes to human-authored multi-turn attacks remains the primary open question (length confound analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:length_independence}).

OOD results reflect attack-side generalization only: benign data is IID, so OOD F1 figures are upper bounds (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}). On WildChat, 9-class FPR reaches 68.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:wildchat_fpr}). This limitation is addressable: a benign adaptation curve (Appendix~\ref{app:benign_mixed}) shows that as few as 10 target-domain benign conversations eliminate FPR without detection degradation, though validation on larger and more diverse OOD benign samples is needed. Residual calibration error (ECE\,=\,0.126--0.141; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}) suggests temperature scaling is needed for deployment.

Three encoders (125M--355M) differ in architecture and pretraining; same-architecture scaling is needed to isolate the capacity effect, and whether findings hold at LLM-scale encoders (7B+) remains open. Only persuasion-mediated multi-turn attacks fall within scope; single-turn token-level attacks~\citep{zou2023gcg}, non-persuasive strategies, and non-text modalities are not addressed. Total pipeline cost is under 4 GPU-hours on a single A100.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work is defensive: it improves detection of multi-turn jailbreak attacks rather than developing new attacks. We acknowledge dual-use risk, as the persuasion taxonomy could inform adversarial actors. We mitigate this by using only synthetic conversations (no real harmful content), withholding attack-generation prompts, and focusing contributions on detection mechanisms. No human subjects were involved; all data will be released under CC BY-NC 4.0.

% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and miss multi-turn manipulation; Llama Guard~3 detects only 1.8\% of multi-turn jailbreaks in our evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}). Multi-turn defenses (DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid}, G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025}) model conversation-level structure but each evaluates on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

Pretrained language understanding does not suffice at base model scale: vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on OOD Deceptive Delight, and GPT-5.5 reaches only 0.191--0.449 F1 with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). The bottleneck is distributional: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a better classifier.

Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD} (Disentangled Persuasion Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants systematically vary training objectives while sharing the same two-stage pipeline (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the contribution is the decomposition, not the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions reveals two orthogonal generalization axes. On the \emph{attack side}, vanilla DeBERTa drops to F1\,=\,0.28--0.31 on OOD attacks; classification DAPT recovers to 0.97--1.0, while MLM collapses under mixed-LLM training (F1\,=\,0.289 $\approx$ vanilla). On the \emph{benign side}, distributional mismatch drives false positives: WildChat FPR reaches 68.1\%, but as few as 10 target-domain benign conversations eliminate it (95\% CI upper 6.1\%). RoBERTa-large (355M) handles ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. Classification supervision is associated with substantially lower prediction variance on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Two-axis generalization decomposition.} We identify two orthogonal generalization challenges in multi-turn jailbreak detection. Attack-side: controlled ablation across thirteen encoder configurations reveals classification DAPT as the most robust variant (vanilla F1\,=\,0.28--0.31 on OOD $\rightarrow$ 0.97--1.0); unsupervised pretraining collapses under mixed-LLM training. Benign-side: 10 target-domain benign conversations eliminate WildChat FPR from 68.1\% to 0\% (95\% CI upper 6.1\%) without detection degradation.

  \item \textbf{Condition-dependent adaptation effectiveness and constraining negative results.} MLM is effective on single-source data but collapses under mixed-LLM training; classification DAPT is robust across conditions. Three supplementary negative results constrain which properties matter: scrambled labels perform comparably to correct labels on template-based attacks (TOST-confirmed on DD and FITD at $n$\,=\,10, $d$\,=\,0.44 on structurally complex ActorAttack), temporal order is irrelevant ($\Delta < 1$\,pp), and label granularity does not affect detection.

  \item \textbf{Practical deployment recipe.} Continued pretraining is 24.3$\times$ faster than LLM-as-judge (Appendix~\ref{app:efficiency}), and 10-shot benign adaptation eliminates domain-specific false positives. No target-domain attack data is required for deployment.
\end{itemize}

